## Intent & Decisions
Let the provider configure the prefill pool of a prefill/decode (PD)
deployment, which the inference API already supports. The user grades
the PR on the smallest diff that adds PD; refactors and anything else
outside that scope count against it and belong in follow-ups.

Settled choices:
- An optional pd attribute holds prefill_resources and
  prefill_autoscaling (min, max, concurrency), mapped to the API
  DeploymentPD message. The existing resources and autoscaling
  attributes keep configuring the decode pool.
- A change to pd updates the deployment in place. hot_load uses
  RequiresReplace because the API treats it as immutable; pd is not.
- Create and update both send Pd, because UpdateDeployment replaces the
  whole specification.
- prefill_autoscaling leaves out capacity_classes. The attribute
  description says the prefill pool uses the API default classes and
  does not inherit autoscaling.capacity_classes. Trade-off: a prefill
  capacity class set outside Terraform is cleared by the next Terraform
  update with no visible diff. Adding the optional attribute later is a
  backward-compatible change.
- Superseded: an earlier amend made pdFromProto return an error when
  the API returned pd without prefill_resources or prefill_autoscaling.
  Copilot review on 093afbd showed that Create returns on any
  setFromDeployment error before its first resp.State.Set, so a
  malformed create response would leave the new deployment untracked
  and impossible for Terraform to destroy. The user chose to remove
  the check rather than change shared Create code. pdFromProto again
  maps nil sub-messages to zero values through the proto getters; the
  deployment stays tracked and Terraform reports an inconsistent
  result instead.
- Copilot also noted that prefill min, max, and concurrency only have
  AtLeast(1), so values above 4294967295 wrap in the uint32 cast. The
  decode autoscaling attributes have the same gap; an upper bound for
  both pools is a follow-up, not part of this PR.
- pdFromProto is a pure mapping: concurrency is null when the API
  returns 0. AtLeast(1) on the attribute makes 0 always mean unset.
  This differs on purpose from the decode read, which checks the prior
  state; matching it would need the prior model passed in.
- After a style pass, the user chose the older file style over the
  fewest lines for three spots: multi-line Validators blocks in the pd
  schema (12 older attributes use them; only hot_load used one line),
  one if per field in ToUpdateRequest_Fields, and a separate
  SetFromDeployment_IncompletePD test instead of a check inside the
  NullPreservation test. That test was later removed with the check.
- scotchneat review on ffe1ecf (changes requested), addressed in this
  commit: ValidateConfig rejects pd unless runtime.engine is
  "dynamo-vllm" (the proto comment on Deployment.pd states that
  constraint); prefill instance_type gets LengthAtLeast(1); a comment
  above the prefill autoscaling casts explains the //nolint:gosec and
  the planned upper-bound follow-up. The engine check is hardcoded on
  purpose although ModifyPlan avoids a hardcoded engine list: the API
  advertises no per-engine PD capability to check against. Decode
  instance_type still has no LengthAtLeast(1); not changed here.
- scotchneat second review on f292187: ValidateConfig also returns
  when pd is unknown, because a pd set from a variable is unknown at
  validation and may still turn out null.
- scotchneat also left a nit asking for a more descriptive name than
  pd, without a suggestion. The name was kept because it matches the
  API field and the proto docs (pd.prefill_*). Renaming is a public
  schema decision for the user.

